      "note": null
    }
  ],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Many languages use one word for *two days ago* and *two days before*; English separates them: **ago** counts back from now and **before** counts back from a time in the past.",
    "grammar": "With the meaning *at an earlier time up to now*, **before** usually goes with the present perfect: *I have seen it before*, not the simple present.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Two syllables with the stress on the second: *bih-FOR*. American speakers say the **r**; British speakers do not."
  },
